docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-471-- The risks instruction no longer says that keyhole techniques and experienced surgeons “make these procedures safe for most suitable candidates”; it now requires small-incision description without comparative safety, pain or recovery claims.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:472:- The BMI summary and bullets now state the MBS rule: BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment; BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-473-- Procedure bullets no longer promise comparative weight loss, “preferred” status, substantial outcomes or least weight loss; they direct the surgeon to explain individual risks, benefits and follow-up.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:474:- The cost direction now uses conditional Medicare/private-health wording, requires any range or estimate to be discussed at consultation, and keeps dollar figures out.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:475:- The FAQ direction now includes Perth cost, Medicare coverage, the 30/30 rule, who may not meet MBS or clinical suitability requirements, 10-year variation and how to assess a surgeon.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:476:- The GLP-1/active-ingredient wording is marked as pending TGA/legal review; if retained, use active-ingredient/class names only and no brand names.
