docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md-181-- **Blocking #2 (BMI 30–35 wording) — BRIEF-INHERITED.** The brief itself mandates this exact framing (`content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:88`: "BMI of 35 or above, or BMI 30–35 with weight-related health conditions"). The MBS-accuracy concern stands, but this is not writer error — the brief needs the same correction. Frame as "fix draft AND brief."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md:182:- **Blocking #3 (hospitals) — reframed: blocked until VERIFIED, do not rewrite either direction.** All five drafts consistently name Mount Private/The Park/Waikiki; this may be newer client info than the brief. Confirm with Marcus/client before changing anything; do not apply the SJOG rewrite suggested above until confirmed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md-183-- **Blocking #6 (CTA "could change things") — downgraded to Should-fix.** Conditional exploration framing; the brief's own reference CTA is in the same register. Style, not compliance.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md-184-- **ADDED Blocking (missed): "life-changing" claim.** Draft: "While it can be life-changing, weight loss surgery is a tool, not a one-off fix." Separate transformational-benefit phrase (AHPRA s133 unreasonable-expectation risk) not covered by the CTA finding. Rewrite: "Weight loss surgery is a tool that works alongside long-term changes, not a one-off fix."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md:185:- **ADDED Blocking (missed): absolute private-health coverage claims.** Draft: "private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component" and "will cover the hospital component." Gold-tier includes the category, but benefits are policy-, waiting-period- and gap-dependent — same cost-certainty class as the fee figures. Rewrite: "private health insurance with appropriate hospital cover (typically gold tier) may contribute to hospital and theatre costs; out-of-pockets vary by fund and policy."
