docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/final-pass-codex-xhigh.md-4-
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/final-pass-codex-xhigh.md-5-Regulatory anchors checked:
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/final-pass-codex-xhigh.md-6-
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/final-pass-codex-xhigh.md:7:- MBS Online item 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass; item 31575 is sleeve gastrectomy; item 31581 is biliopancreatic diversion with or without duodenal switch. MBS explanatory note TN.8.29 says clinically severe obesity generally means BMI 40+, or BMI 35+ with major medical co-morbidities, with population variation and final clinical judgment by the surgeon. Sources: https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/fullDisplay.cfm?q=31572&type=item, https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/fullDisplay.cfm?q=31575&type=item, https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/fullDisplay.cfm?q=31581&type=item.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/final-pass-codex-xhigh.md-8-- Ahpra/National Law s133 advertising restrictions include false or misleading advertising, testimonials or purported testimonials, unreasonable expectation of beneficial treatment, and encouraging indiscriminate or unnecessary use of regulated health services. Source: https://www.ahpra.gov.au/Resources/Advertising-hub/Advertising-guidelines-and-other-guidance/Advertising-and-the-law.aspx.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/final-pass-codex-xhigh.md:9:- TGA guidance says public advertising must not directly or indirectly reference prescription medicines or prescription-medicine classes, including examples such as GLP-1 receptor agonists and weight-loss injections, when advertising health services. Sources: https://www.tga.gov.au/resources/guidance/complying-restrictions-advertising-prescription-medicines-public and https://www.tga.gov.au/resources/guidance/advertising-health-services-involve-therapeutic-goods.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/final-pass-codex-xhigh.md:10:- PrivateHealth.gov.au confirms weight loss surgery is a Gold-tier hospital category, but coverage and out-of-pocket exposure still depend on the policy, waiting periods, hospital agreements, excesses/co-payments, and gap arrangements. Sources: https://www.privatehealth.gov.au/health_insurance/howitworks/producttiers.htm, https://www.privatehealth.gov.au/health_insurance/howitworks/waiting_periods.htm, https://www.privatehealth.gov.au/health_insurance/howitworks/out_of_pocket.htm.
