     1	# Gastric Sleeve Perth — content review
     2	
     3	**Verdict: Rework** · 8 must-fix · 12 should-fix · 4 minor
     4	
     5	**Why:** Safety, Medicare eligibility, hospital verification, cost certainty and outcome-claim issues need correction before publication.
     6	
     7	**Length:** current body 1,844 words vs brief target 1,200-1,500 words, including FAQs.
     8	
     9	## Must fix before publish
    10	
    11	1. **AHPRA risk: the opening claim overstates safety and effectiveness**
    12	
    13	> Yet in the right hands, gastric sleeve surgery is a safe, powerful, and lasting weight loss tool.
    14	
    15	> For some people, gastric sleeve surgery can support substantial long-term weight loss, but it is major surgery with risks, and suitability needs to be assessed by a bariatric surgeon.
    16	
    17	Why it matters: "Safe, powerful, and lasting" creates an unbalanced beneficial expectation for a regulated health service, so risk balance needs to sit in the same sentence.
    18	
    19	2. **MBS/BMI eligibility is not accurate enough for Medicare item 31575 [also fix the brief]**
    20	
    21	> A sleeve gastrectomy is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30-35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux.
    22	
    23	> For Medicare item 31575, MBS eligibility generally requires a BMI of 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment.
    24	
    25	Why it matters: The draft's BMI 30-35 framing may be a broader clinical eligibility statement, but it should not sit unqualified on a page that also discusses Medicare item 31575; the writer followed the brief as instructed, so the brief also needs correction.
    26	
    27	3. **Hospital names must be verified with the client before changing either direction**
    28	
    29	> You'll spend 1-2 nights recuperating at [<u>Mount Private Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth.
    30	
    31	> Most patients spend around two nights in hospital at [the hospital location to be verified with the client], with discharge timing depending on recovery and clinical advice.
    32	
    33	Why it matters: The hospital names conflict with the brief, but the draft may reflect newer client information, so verify the correct hospital name(s) with the client before changing either direction.
    34	
    35	4. **The cost section needs consultation-based, no-dollar wording**
    36	
    37	> **How much does a gastric sleeve cost in Perth?**
    38	>
    39	> Gastric sleeve surgery cost varies depending on your level of private health insurance cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital and theatre charges, and any out-of-pocket gap that applies to your situation. Medicare item number 31575 does attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component. Your surgeon’s fee for a primary laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy is \$4,000 for both insured and uninsured patients, rising to \$5,000 for a complex case. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients. A full breakdown of costs, funding pathways, and pre-surgical clinical fees can be found here.
    40	
    41	> Total out-of-pocket costs vary depending on private health cover, surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital charges, complexity and any applicable gap. Medicare item 31575 may attract a rebate when MBS requirements are met, while private health insurance may contribute to hospital and theatre costs depending on the policy, waiting periods and fund rules. Any current fee range should be discussed at consultation, together with the components that apply to your circumstances; ask the team to explain available funding pathways and review the current gastric sleeve cost guide before deciding whether to proceed.
    42	
    43	Why it matters: The brief explicitly says not to quote a single dollar figure. Costs, rebates, private-health benefits and funding pathways need current, approved disclosure and should be framed as matters to discuss at consultation.
    44	
    45	5. **Appetite and effectiveness wording are too certain**
    46	
    47	> Gastric sleeve surgery is highly effective for weight loss for two key reasons: first, because your stomach is smaller, you feel fuller, faster. Secondly, because a reduced stomach size minimises your body’s production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’), after gastric sleeve surgery, your appetite will also be reduced.
    48	
    49	> The operation reduces stomach capacity, which may help some people feel full with smaller portions. It also removes part of the stomach that produces much of the body’s ghrelin, a hormone involved in hunger, so appetite changes vary between people in degree and duration.
    50	
    51	Why it matters: "Will also be reduced" reads like an outcome guarantee, while the rewrite keeps the mechanism and qualifies individual variation.
    52	
    53	6. **The "life-changing" phrase creates a transformational-benefit claim**
    54	
    55	> While it can be life-changing, weight loss surgery is a tool, not a one-off fix.
    56	
    57	> Weight loss surgery is a tool that works alongside long-term changes, not a one-off fix.
    58	
    59	Why it matters: "Life-changing" risks creating an unreasonable expectation of benefit under health advertising rules, even when the surrounding sentence is otherwise balanced.
    60	
    61	7. **The private-health coverage claim is repeated in the Medicare FAQ**
    62	
    63	> **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item number 31575 attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the hospital component. A full fee breakdown is available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.
    64	
    65	> **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item 31575 may attract a rebate when the MBS requirements are met, but Medicare does not cover every fee. Hospital, theatre, surgeon and anaesthetist costs, private-health benefits and out-of-pocket gaps depend on your circumstances and policy, so confirm current details with your surgeon’s office and insurer. A consultation can help you understand which costs and rebates apply to you.
    66	
    67	Why it matters: The cost-section replacement covers the earlier insurance claim; this later FAQ repeats the absolute claim. Coverage depends on fund rules, policy settings, waiting periods and gap arrangements, so the copy should not imply automatic cover.
    68	
    69	8. **Residual AHPRA-risk wording remains in the draft**
    70	
    71	These occurrences were not covered by the first-pass fixes. Replace each exact draft passage:
    72	
    73	> It is typically performed ‘laparoscopically’ via keyhole surgery, which tends to mean an easier recovery than some more invasive procedures.
    74	
    75	> It is typically performed laparoscopically, via keyhole surgery; recovery depends on the procedure and the individual, and your surgeon will provide tailored advice.
    76	
    77	> Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
    78	
    79	> Weight loss may occur after surgery; clinical averages are reported as 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months in some studies, but individual results vary and no result can be guaranteed.
    80	
    81	> Reduced appetite; the procedure reduces stomach size and lowers ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’) production, so smaller portions feel more satisfying.
    82	
    83	> Reduced appetite may occur because the procedure reduces stomach size and lowers ghrelin production; individual experiences vary, including whether smaller portions feel more satisfying.
    84	
    85	> Keyhole approach; means less scarring, generally less post-operative pain, and a shorter hospital stay (and recovery time) than open surgery.
    86	
    87	> Keyhole surgery uses smaller incisions than open surgery; scarring, pain, hospital stay and recovery vary by individual and surgical circumstances.
    88	
    89	> Gastric sleeve surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail.
    90	
    91	> Gastric sleeve surgery is major surgery with risks that your surgeon will discuss with you in detail, including risks that may be specific to your health.
    92	
    93	> Staple line leak; though it is a serious potential complication and may require further treatment, it only occurs in ~1–3% of cases.
    94	
    95	> Staple line leak is a serious potential complication that may require further treatment; reported rates vary by patient and clinical setting, so ask your surgeon how this risk is monitored and managed.
    96	
    97	> Reflux or heartburn can occur or (if pre-existing) worsen after a sleeve. In most people it settles, though for others it can be persistent.
    98	
    99	> Reflux or heartburn can occur or worsen after a sleeve. Some people’s symptoms settle, while others may have persistent symptoms; discuss your reflux history with your surgeon.
   100	
   101	> Hair thinning in the months after surgery is typically linked to rapid weight loss and reduced nutritional intake; it’s usually temporary and improves as your intake stabilises.
   102	
   103	> Hair thinning can occur in the months after surgery and may be associated with rapid weight loss or reduced nutritional intake; ask your care team about persistent symptoms or nutritional concerns.
   104	
   105	> **4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?** A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway unchanged. A gastric bypass both reduces the stomach *and* reroutes the small intestine, which can cause some nutrient malabsorption. The bypass may produce slightly greater weight loss and tends to suit patients with significant reflux better.
   106	
   107	> **4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?** A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size without rerouting the small intestine. A gastric bypass reduces stomach size and reroutes part of the small intestine, so the procedures can have different nutritional and weight-loss effects. Reflux history, BMI, other health conditions and surgeon assessment all contribute to the choice; neither procedure is right for everyone.
   108	
   109	> **6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right for me?** Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon, as a sleeve can actually worsen symptoms in some patients. For those with significant GORD, a gastric bypass is often the more suitable option as it tends to improve reflux rather than aggravate it.
