    36	**<span class="mark">Gastric sleeve surgery in Perth, a straightforward
    37	guide from a team you can trust</span>**
    38	
    39	<span class="mark">If you are one of the [<u>75% of West
    40	Australians</u>](https://www.health.wa.gov.au/~/media/Corp/Documents/Health-for/Nutrition-monitoring-survey-series/2022/Weight-and-Health_FINAL.pdf)
    41	living with overweight or obesity and are exploring different types of
    42	bariatric surgery, you may be considering a sleeve gastrectomy (gastric
    43	sleeve surgery). Weight loss surgery is a big decision, often after
    44	years of unsuccessful weight loss and regain and all the emotional
    45	challenges that accompany it. Yet in the right hands, gastric sleeve
    46	surgery is a safe, powerful, and lasting weight loss tool.</span>
    47	
    48	### **<span class="mark">What is a gastric sleeve?</span>**
    49	
    50	<span class="mark">A sleeve gastrectomy, commonly known as gastric
    51	sleeve surgery, is a surgical procedure that reduces the size of your
    52	stomach by up to 75-80%. The ‘sleeve’ is created by constructing a
    53	narrow banana-shaped tube from your stomach. It is typically performed
    54	‘laparoscopically’ via keyhole surgery, which tends to mean an easier
    55	recovery than some more invasive procedures.</span>
    56	
    57	<span class="mark">Gastric sleeve surgery is highly effective for weight
    58	loss for two key reasons: first, because your stomach is smaller, you
    59	feel fuller, faster. Secondly, because a reduced stomach size minimises
    60	your body’s production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’), after gastric
    61	sleeve surgery, your appetite will also be reduced.</span>
    62	
    63	<span class="mark">As the gastric sleeve procedure is permanent, your
    64	surgeon will help you understand the risks and benefits, and your
    65	suitability based on your weight and whether or not you are experiencing
    66	any associated health conditions.</span>
    67	
    68	<table>
    69	<colgroup>
    70	<col style="width: 100%" />
    71	</colgroup>
    72	<thead>
    73	<tr>
    74	<th style="text-align: left;"><p><strong><mark>Gastric sleeve vs Gastric
    75	bypass</mark></strong></p>
    76	<p><mark>Unlike a gastric bypass, which reroutes the small intestine to
    77	reduce calorie absorption, a sleeve gastrectomy does not alter how your
    78	digestive system functions. Food still travels and is digested as it
    79	always has, just more slowly.</mark></p>
    80	<p><mark>Another key difference between the gastric sleeve and gastric
    81	band is that the sleeve</mark> <a
    82	href="https://www.obesitysurgerywa.com.au/bariatric-dietitian-murdoch-perth/"><mark>restricts
    83	only the amount</mark></a> <mark>of food <a
    84	href="https://www.obesitysurgerywa.com.au/bariatric-dietitian-murdoch-perth/">you
    85	can consume</a>, not the types of foods (as is the case with the gastric
    86	band procedure).</mark></p></th>
    87	</tr>
    88	</thead>
    89	<tbody>
    90	</tbody>
    91	</table>
    92	
    93	### **<span class="mark">Am I eligible for gastric sleeve surgery?</span>**
    94	
    95	<span class="mark">A sleeve gastrectomy is generally considered suitable
    96	for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30–35 with associated
    97	weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood
    98	pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux.</span>
    99	
   100	<span class="mark">Calculate your BMI.  
   101	  
   102	Your BMI is a great starting point, however your surgeon will take the
   103	time to assess your whole health picture to determine if you are a good
   104	candidate for a gastric sleeve.</span>
   105	
   106	**<span class="mark">You may also be a candidate for a gastric sleeve if
   107	you:</span>**
   108	
   109	- <span class="mark">have tried to lose weight previously through diet
   110	  and lifestyle changes without lasting success</span>
   111	
   112	- <span class="mark">are open to making long-term dietary changes and
   113	  committing to ongoing aftercare including post-op check ins and
   114	  dietitian follow-ups</span>
   115	
   116	- <span class="mark">are not experiencing any untreated health
   117	  conditions that would make surgery unsafe (some conditions may need
   118	  management first rather than ruling surgery out altogether)</span>
   119	
   120	<span class="mark">While it can be life-changing, weight loss surgery is
   121	a tool, not a one-off fix. The best way to explore your options is to
   122	schedule an initial consultation with a</span> <span class="mark">Perth
   123	bariatric surgeon.</span>
   124	
   125	### <span class="mark"> **Gastric sleeve surgery in Perth: how it works** </span>
   126	
   127	1.  <span class="mark">**Initial consultation and assessment**  
   128	    Here you’ll meet your surgeon, talk through your medical history and
   129	    weight loss goals, ask as many questions as you like, and receive a
   130	    professional opinion about whether a gastric sleeve is suitable for
   131	    you and your lifestyle.  
   132	    </span>
   133	
   134	2.  <span class="mark">**Pre-surgery preparation**  
   135	    You may need to follow a specific pre-op diet to shrink your liver
   136	    and reduce surgical risk (typically 2–4 weeks). You won’t do this
   137	    alone!</span> <span class="mark">Obesity Surgery WA’s dietitians and
   138	    nursing staff are on-hand to support you throughout.  
   139	    </span>
   140	
   141	3.  <span class="mark">**Procedure day  **
   142	    A sleeve gastrectomy requires a general anaesthesia and is performed
   143	    via keyhole surgery. Total surgical time is ~45–60 minutes. Your
   144	    surgeon removes ~75–80% of your stomach and seals the new ‘sleeve’
   145	    with a staple line.  
   146	    </span>
   147	
   148	4.  <span class="mark">**Hospital recovery**  
   149	    You’ll spend 1-2 nights recuperating at [<u>Mount Private
   150	    Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park
   151	    Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki
   152	    Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth.
   153	    Guided and monitored by nursing and dietetic staff, you’ll be on
   154	    fluids to start before being cleared to head home.  
   155	    </span>
   156	
   157	5.  <span class="mark">**Returning to work and daily life**  
   158	    Most light daily activities can usually be resumed gradually within
   159	    the first fortnight after a gastric sleeve. You’ll be prescribed ~2
   160	    weeks’ rest from desk work, however physical or manual labour will
   161	    require closer to 4–6 weeks.  
   162	    </span>
   163	
   164	6.  <span class="mark">**Ongoing support**  
   165	    Progression from fluids only → thicker liquids and purees → regular
   166	    textures will take place with the support of your</span>
   167	    <span class="mark">dietitian. Plus you’ll need ongoing reviews
   168	    (regularly post-op then annually) and bloodwork done to protect your
   169	    nutrition and results long-term.</span>
   170	
   171	### <span class="mark">**Benefits of gastric sleeve surgery**  Beyond weight loss alone, gastric sleeve surgery offers a range of benefits that can improve both your physical health and day-to-day quality of life. </span>
   172	
   173	1.  <span class="mark">Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated
   174	    clinical average of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months,
   175	    though individual results vary.</span>
   176	
   177	2.  <span class="mark">Reduced appetite; the procedure reduces stomach
   178	    size and lowers ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’) production, so smaller
   179	    portions feel more satisfying.</span>
   180	
   181	3.  <span class="mark">Improvement in weight-related conditions such as
   182	    type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, and joint pain,
   183	    though outcomes will differ between individuals.</span>
   184	
   185	4.  <span class="mark">No intestinal rerouting; food travels through the
   186	    digestive tract in its normal order, meaning no malabsorption as is
   187	    seen with a bypass.</span>
   188	
   189	5.  <span class="mark">Keyhole approach; means less scarring, generally
   190	    less post-operative pain, and a shorter hospital stay (and recovery
   191	    time) than open surgery.</span>
   192	
   193	6.  <span class="mark">Single permanent procedure; no implanted device
   194	    to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band.  
   195	    </span>
   196	
   197	### **<span class="mark">Risks of gastric sleeve surgery</span>**
   198	
   199	### <span class="mark">Gastric sleeve surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail. </span>
   200	
   201	1.  <span class="mark">Staple line leak; though it is a serious
   202	    potential complication and may require further treatment, it only
   203	    occurs in ~1–3% of cases.</span>
   204	
   205	2.  <span class="mark">Reflux or heartburn can occur or (if
   206	    pre-existing) worsen after a sleeve. In most people it settles,
   207	    though for others it can be persistent.</span>
   208	
   209	3.  <span class="mark">Nutritional deficiencies (particularly vitamin
   210	    B12, iron, and vitamin D) are characteristic of the reduced stomach
   211	    size, meaning vitamin supplements and regular blood tests are
   212	    recommended long-term for your health.</span>
   213	
   214	4.  <span class="mark">Hair thinning in the months after surgery is
   215	    typically linked to rapid weight loss and reduced nutritional
   216	    intake; it’s usually temporary and improves as your intake
   217	    stabilises.</span>
   218	
   219	5.  <span class="mark">Weight regain is also possible if your eating
   220	    habits drift back to pre-operative ‘normal’ and/or your follow-up
   221	    care is interrupted or lapses; the success of a gastric sleeve works
   222	    best alongside continued care and support.</span>
   223	
   224	6.  <span class="mark">General surgical risks include bleeding,
   225	    infection, blood clots, and anaesthetic reactions, as with any
   226	    operation.</span>
   227	
   228	<span class="mark">**How much does a gastric sleeve cost in Perth?  **
   229	  
   230	Gastric sleeve surgery cost varies depending on your level of private
   231	health insurance cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital and
   232	theatre charges, and any out-of-pocket gap that applies to your
   233	situation. [<u>Medicare item number
   234	31575</u>](https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/fullDisplay.cfm?type=item&q=31575&qt=ItemID)
   235	does attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private
   236	health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the
   237	hospital and theatre component. Your surgeon’s fee for a primary
   238	laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy is \$4,000 for both insured and
   239	uninsured patients, rising to \$5,000 for a complex case. For those
   240	without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a
   241	concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain
   242	circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain
   243	psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing
   244	options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients. A
   245	full breakdown of costs, funding pathways, and pre-surgical clinical
   246	fees</span> <span class="mark">can be found here.</span>
   247	
   248	### **<span class="mark">Conversion CTA headline</span>**
   249	
   250	<span class="mark">Ready to explore whether a gastric sleeve could
   251	change things for you? Let's talk.  
   252	Alt  
   253	Ready to find out if a gastric sleeve is right for you? Book a
   254	consultation today.</span>
   255	
   256	### **<span class="mark">FAQs (8 questions)</span>**
   257	
   258	<span class="mark">**1. Is the gastric sleeve permanent, and can it be
   259	reversed?  **
   260	Yes, a gastric sleeve is permanent; a portion of your stomach is removed
   261	and cannot be reattached. However, if further intervention is needed
   262	down the track, a sleeve *can* be converted to a gastric bypass or
   263	another procedure. Your surgeon will discuss long-term expectations with
   264	you during your consultation.</span>
   265	
   266	<span class="mark">**2. How much weight will I actually lose?  **
   267	On average, gastric sleeve patients lose around 60–70% of their excess
   268	body weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary. Factors
   269	like starting weight, age, diet, and lifestyle will influence outcomes.
   270	Some weight regain over time is not uncommon and long-term success is
   271	generally supported by ongoing follow-up with your surgical and dietetic
   272	team.</span>
   273	
   274	<span class="mark">**3. Will I have to take vitamins for the rest of my
   275	life?  **
   276	Yes, a lifelong commitment to vitamins and supplements is strongly
   277	recommended. Although the sleeve doesn't reroute digestion, eating
   278	significantly less makes it harder to meet nutritional needs through
   279	food alone. Iron, B12, vitamin D, and calcium are among the most
   280	commonly monitored nutrients. Your dietitian will advise you on
   281	supplements, and regular blood tests help keep levels on track.</span>
   282	
   283	<span class="mark">**4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric
   284	bypass?  **
   285	A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway
   286	unchanged. A gastric bypass both reduces the stomach *and* reroutes the
   287	small intestine, which can cause some nutrient malabsorption. The bypass
   288	may produce slightly greater weight loss and tends to suit patients with
   289	significant reflux better.</span>
   290	
   291	<span class="mark">**5. What does recovery actually look like week to
   292	week?  **
   293	Your first 1-2 days post op are spent in hospital on clear fluids. Week
   294	one is rest at home, sipping carefully and managing fatigue. By weeks
   295	two to three, your diet progresses from fluids to pureed and soft foods.
   296	Light daily activity and desk-style work can resume within a fortnight,
   297	whereas more physical activity and manual work should wait for four to
   298	six weeks.</span>
   299	
   300	<span class="mark">**6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right
   301	for me?  **
   302	Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon,
   303	as a sleeve can actually worsen symptoms in some patients. For those
   304	with significant GORD, a gastric bypass is often the more suitable
   305	option as it tends to improve reflux rather than aggravate it.</span>
   306	
   307	<span class="mark">**7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?  **
   308	[<u>Medicare item number
   309	31575</u>](https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/fullDisplay.cfm?type=item&q=31575&qt=ItemID)
   310	attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of
   311	the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost
   312	and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health
   313	insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the
   314	hospital component. A</span> <span class="mark">full fee breakdown is
   315	available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.</span>
   316	
   317	<span class="mark">**8. Do I need a GP referral to be seen?  **
   318	A referral isn't required to make an enquiry or book a consultation, but
   319	having one is worthwhile. It allows Medicare rebates to apply to your
   320	specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the
   321	appointment. It also ensures your surgeon has your relevant medical
   322	history from the outset. If you don't yet have a referral, our team can
   323	advise you on next steps.</span>

