    10	
    11	By default, layouts and pages are [Server Components](https://react.dev/reference/rsc/server-components), which lets you fetch data and render parts of your UI on the server, optionally cache the result, and stream it to the client. When you need interactivity or browser APIs, you can use [Client Components](https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-client) to layer in functionality.
    12	
    13	This page explains how Server and Client Components work in Next.js and when to use them, with examples of how to compose them together in your application.
    14	
    15	## When to use Server and Client Components?
    16	
    17	The client and server environments have different capabilities. Server and Client components allow you to run logic in each environment depending on your use case.
    18	
    19	Use **Client Components** when you need:
    20	
    21	- [State](https://react.dev/learn/managing-state) and [event handlers](https://react.dev/learn/responding-to-events). E.g. `onClick`, `onChange`.
    22	- [Lifecycle logic](https://react.dev/learn/lifecycle-of-reactive-effects). E.g. `useEffect`.
    23	- Browser-only APIs. E.g. `localStorage`, `window`, `Navigator.geolocation`, etc.
    24	- [Custom hooks](https://react.dev/learn/reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks).
    25	
    26	Use **Server Components** when you need:
    27	
    28	- Fetch data from databases or APIs close to the source.
    29	- Use API keys, tokens, and other secrets without exposing them to the client.
    30	- Reduce the amount of JavaScript sent to the browser.
    31	- Improve the [First Contentful Paint (FCP)](https://web.dev/fcp/), and stream content progressively to the client.
    32	
    33	For example, the `<Page>` component is a Server Component that fetches data about a post, and passes it as props to the `<LikeButton>` which handles client-side interactivity.
    34	
    35	```tsx filename="app/[id]/page.tsx" highlight={1,17} switcher
    36	import LikeButton from '@/app/ui/like-button'
    37	import { getPost } from '@/lib/data'
    38	
    39	export default async function Page({
    40	  params,
    41	}: {
    42	  params: Promise<{ id: string }>
    43	}) {
    44	  const { id } = await params
    45	  const post = await getPost(id)
    46	
    47	  return (
    48	    <div>
    49	      <main>
    50	        <h1>{post.title}</h1>
    51	        {/* ... */}
    52	        <LikeButton likes={post.likes} />
    53	      </main>
    54	    </div>
    55	  )
    56	}
    57	```
    58	
    59	```jsx filename="app/[id]/page.js" highlight={1,12} switcher
    60	import LikeButton from '@/app/ui/like-button'
    61	import { getPost } from '@/lib/data'
    62	
    63	export default async function Page({ params }) {
    64	  const post = await getPost(params.id)
    65	
    66	  return (
    67	    <div>
    68	      <main>
    69	        <h1>{post.title}</h1>
    70	        {/* ... */}
    71	        <LikeButton likes={post.likes} />
    72	      </main>
    73	    </div>
    74	  )
    75	}
    76	```
    77	
    78	```tsx filename="app/ui/like-button.tsx" highlight={1} switcher
    79	'use client'
    80	
    81	import { useState } from 'react'
    82	
    83	export default function LikeButton({ likes }: { likes: number }) {
    84	  // ...
    85	}
    86	```
    87	
    88	```jsx filename="app/ui/like-button.js" highlight={1} switcher
    89	'use client'
    90	
    91	import { useState } from 'react'
    92	
    93	export default function LikeButton({ likes }) {
    94	  // ...
    95	}
    96	```
    97	
    98	## How do Server and Client Components work in Next.js?
    99	
   100	### On the server
   101	
   102	On the server, Next.js uses React's APIs to orchestrate rendering. The rendering work is split into chunks, by individual route segments ([layouts and pages](/docs/app/getting-started/layouts-and-pages)):
   103	
   104	- **Server Components** are rendered into a special data format called the React Server Component Payload (RSC Payload).
   105	- **Client Components** and the RSC Payload are used to [prerender](/docs/app/glossary#prerendering) HTML.
   106	
   107	> **What is the React Server Component Payload (RSC)?**
   108	>
   109	> The RSC Payload is a compact binary representation of the rendered React Server Components tree. It's used by React on the client to update the browser's DOM. The RSC Payload contains:
   110	>
   111	> - The rendered result of Server Components
   112	> - Placeholders for where Client Components should be rendered and references to their JavaScript files
   113	> - Any props passed from a Server Component to a Client Component
   114	
   115	### On the client (first load)
   116	
   117	Then, on the client:
   118	
   119	1. **HTML** is used to immediately show a fast non-interactive preview of the route to the user.
   120	2. **RSC Payload** is used to reconcile the Client and Server Component trees.
   121	3. **JavaScript** is used to hydrate Client Components and make the application interactive.
   122	
   123	> **What is hydration?**
   124	>
   125	> Hydration is React's process for attaching [event handlers](https://react.dev/learn/responding-to-events) to the DOM, to make the static HTML interactive.
   126	
   127	### Subsequent Navigations
   128	
   129	On subsequent navigations:
   130	
   131	- The **RSC Payload** is prefetched and cached for instant navigation.
   132	- **Client Components** are rendered entirely on the client, without the server-rendered HTML.
   133	
   134	## Examples
   135	
   136	### Using Client Components
   137	
   138	You can create a Client Component by adding the [`"use client"`](https://react.dev/reference/react/use-client) directive at the top of the file, above your imports.
   139	
   140	```tsx filename="app/ui/counter.tsx" highlight={1} switcher
   141	'use client'
   142	
   143	import { useState } from 'react'
   144	
   145	export default function Counter() {
   146	  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
   147	
   148	  return (
   149	    <div>
   150	      <p>{count} likes</p>
   151	      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click me</button>
   152	    </div>
   153	  )
   154	}
   155	```
   156	
   157	```jsx filename="app/ui/counter.js" highlight={1} switcher
   158	'use client'
   159	
   160	import { useState } from 'react'
   161	
   162	export default function Counter() {
   163	  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
   164	
   165	  return (
   166	    <div>
   167	      <p>{count} likes</p>
   168	      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click me</button>
   169	    </div>
   170	  )
   171	}
   172	```
   173	
   174	`"use client"` is used to declare a **boundary** between the Server and Client module graphs (trees).
   175	
   176	Once a file is marked with `"use client"`, **all its imports and child components are considered part of the client bundle**. This means you don't need to add the directive to every component that is intended for the client.
   177	
   178	### Reducing JS bundle size
   179	
   180	To reduce the size of your client JavaScript bundles, add `'use client'` to specific interactive components instead of marking large parts of your UI as Client Components.
   181	
   182	For example, the `<Layout>` component contains mostly static elements like a logo and navigation links, but includes an interactive search bar. `<Search />` is interactive and needs to be a Client Component, however, the rest of the layout can remain a Server Component.
   183	
   184	```tsx filename="app/layout.tsx" highlight={12} switcher
   185	// Client Component
   186	import Search from './search'
   187	// Server Component
   188	import Logo from './logo'
   189	
   190	// Layout is a Server Component by default
   191	export default function Layout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
   192	  return (
   193	    <>
   194	      <nav>
   195	        <Logo />
   196	        <Search />
   197	      </nav>
   198	      <main>{children}</main>
   199	    </>
   200	  )
   201	}
   202	```
   203	
   204	```jsx filename="app/layout.js" highlight={12} switcher
   205	// Client Component
   206	import Search from './search'
   207	// Server Component
   208	import Logo from './logo'
   209	
   210	// Layout is a Server Component by default
   211	export default function Layout({ children }) {
   212	  return (
   213	    <>
   214	      <nav>
   215	        <Logo />
   216	        <Search />
   217	      </nav>
   218	      <main>{children}</main>
   219	    </>
   220	  )
   221	}
   222	```
   223	
   224	```tsx filename="app/ui/search.tsx" highlight={1} switcher
   225	'use client'
   226	
   227	export default function Search() {
   228	  // ...
   229	}
   230	```
   231	
   232	```jsx filename="app/ui/search.js" highlight={1} switcher
   233	'use client'
   234	
   235	export default function Search() {
   236	  // ...
   237	}
   238	```
   239	
   240	### Passing data from Server to Client Components
   241	
   242	You can pass data from Server Components to Client Components using props.
   243	
   244	```tsx filename="app/[id]/page.tsx" highlight={1,12} switcher
   245	import LikeButton from '@/app/ui/like-button'
   246	import { getPost } from '@/lib/data'
   247	
   248	export default async function Page({
   249	  params,
   250	}: {
   251	  params: Promise<{ id: string }>
   252	}) {
   253	  const { id } = await params
   254	  const post = await getPost(id)
   255	
   256	  return <LikeButton likes={post.likes} />
   257	}
   258	```
   259	
   260	```jsx filename="app/[id]/page.js" highlight={1,7} switcher
   261	import LikeButton from '@/app/ui/like-button'
   262	import { getPost } from '@/lib/data'
   263	
   264	export default async function Page({ params }) {
   265	  const post = await getPost(params.id)
   266	
   267	  return <LikeButton likes={post.likes} />
   268	}
   269	```
   270	
   271	```tsx filename="app/ui/like-button.tsx" highlight={1} switcher
   272	'use client'
   273	
   274	export default function LikeButton({ likes }: { likes: number }) {
   275	  // ...
   276	}
   277	```
   278	
   279	```jsx filename="app/ui/like-button.js" highlight={1} switcher
   280	'use client'
   281	
   282	export default function LikeButton({ likes }) {
   283	  // ...
   284	}
   285	```
   286	
   287	Alternatively, you can stream data from a Server Component to a Client Component with the [`use` API](https://react.dev/reference/react/use). See an [example](/docs/app/getting-started/fetching-data#streaming-data-with-the-use-api).
   288	
   289	> **Good to know**: Props passed to Client Components need to be [serializable](https://react.dev/reference/react/use-server#serializable-parameters-and-return-values) by React.
   290	
   291	### Interleaving Server and Client Components
   292	
   293	You can pass Server Components as a prop to a Client Component. This allows you to visually nest server-rendered UI within Client components.
   294	
   295	A common pattern is to use `children` to create a _slot_ in a `<ClientComponent>`. For example, a `<Cart>` component that fetches data on the server, inside a `<Modal>` component that uses client state to toggle visibility.
   296	
   297	```tsx filename="app/ui/modal.tsx" switcher
   298	'use client'
   299	
   300	export default function Modal({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
   301	  return <div>{children}</div>
   302	}
   303	```
   304	
   305	```jsx filename="app/ui/modal.js" switcher
   306	'use client'
   307	
   308	export default function Modal({ children }) {
   309	  return <div>{children}</div>
   310	}
   311	```
   312	
   313	Then, in a parent Server Component (e.g.`<Page>`), you can pass a `<Cart>` as the child of the `<Modal>`:
   314	
   315	```tsx filename="app/page.tsx"  highlight={7} switcher
   316	import Modal from './ui/modal'
   317	import Cart from './ui/cart'
   318	
   319	export default function Page() {
   320	  return (
   321	    <Modal>
   322	      <Cart />
   323	    </Modal>
   324	  )
   325	}
   326	```
   327	
   328	```jsx filename="app/page.js" highlight={7} switcher
   329	import Modal from './ui/modal'
   330	import Cart from './ui/cart'
   331	
   332	export default function Page() {
   333	  return (
   334	    <Modal>
   335	      <Cart />
   336	    </Modal>
   337	  )
   338	}
   339	```
   340	
   341	In this pattern, all Server Components will be rendered on the server ahead of time, including those as props. The resulting RSC payload will contain references of where Client Components should be rendered within the component tree.
   342	
   343	### Context providers
   344	
   345	[React context](https://react.dev/learn/passing-data-deeply-with-context) is commonly used to share global state like the current theme. However, React context is not supported in Server Components.
   346	
   347	To use context, create a Client Component that accepts `children`:
   348	
   349	```tsx filename="app/theme-provider.tsx" switcher
   350	'use client'
   351	
   352	import { createContext } from 'react'
   353	
   354	export const ThemeContext = createContext({})
   355	
   356	export default function ThemeProvider({
   357	  children,
   358	}: {
   359	  children: React.ReactNode
   360	}) {
   361	  return <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">{children}</ThemeContext.Provider>
   362	}
   363	```
   364	
   365	```jsx filename="app/theme-provider.js" switcher
   366	'use client'
   367	
   368	import { createContext } from 'react'
   369	
   370	export const ThemeContext = createContext({})
   371	
   372	export default function ThemeProvider({ children }) {
   373	  return <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">{children}</ThemeContext.Provider>
   374	}
   375	```
   376	
   377	Then, import it into a Server Component (e.g. `layout`):
   378	
   379	```tsx filename="app/layout.tsx" switcher
   380	import ThemeProvider from './theme-provider'
   381	
   382	export default function RootLayout({
   383	  children,
   384	}: {
   385	  children: React.ReactNode
   386	}) {
   387	  return (
   388	    <html>
   389	      <body>
   390	        <ThemeProvider>{children}</ThemeProvider>
   391	      </body>
   392	    </html>
   393	  )
   394	}
   395	```
   396	
   397	```jsx filename="app/layout.js" switcher
   398	import ThemeProvider from './theme-provider'
   399	
   400	export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
   401	  return (
   402	    <html>
   403	      <body>
   404	        <ThemeProvider>{children}</ThemeProvider>
   405	      </body>
   406	    </html>
   407	  )
   408	}
   409	```
   410	
   411	Your Server Component will now be able to directly render your provider, and all other Client Components throughout your app will be able to consume this context.
   412	
   413	> **Good to know**: You should render providers as deep as possible in the tree – notice how `ThemeProvider` only wraps `{children}` instead of the entire `<html>` document. This makes it easier for Next.js to optimize the static parts of your Server Components.
   414	
   415	### Third-party components
   416	
   417	When using a third-party component that relies on client-only features, you can wrap it in a Client Component to ensure it works as expected.
   418	
   419	For example, the `<Carousel />` can be imported from the `acme-carousel` package. This component uses `useState`, but it doesn't yet have the `"use client"` directive.
   420	
   421	If you use `<Carousel />` within a Client Component, it will work as expected:
   422	
   423	```tsx filename="app/gallery.tsx" switcher
   424	'use client'
   425	
   426	import { useState } from 'react'
   427	import { Carousel } from 'acme-carousel'
   428	
   429	export default function Gallery() {
   430	  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false)
   431	
   432	  return (
   433	    <div>
   434	      <button onClick={() => setIsOpen(true)}>View pictures</button>
   435	      {/* Works, since Carousel is used within a Client Component */}
   436	      {isOpen && <Carousel />}
   437	    </div>
   438	  )
   439	}
   440	```
   441	
   442	```jsx filename="app/gallery.js" switcher
   443	'use client'
   444	
   445	import { useState } from 'react'
   446	import { Carousel } from 'acme-carousel'
   447	
   448	export default function Gallery() {
   449	  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false)
   450	
   451	  return (
   452	    <div>
   453	      <button onClick={() => setIsOpen(true)}>View pictures</button>
   454	      {/*  Works, since Carousel is used within a Client Component */}
   455	      {isOpen && <Carousel />}
   456	    </div>
   457	  )
   458	}
   459	```
   460	
   461	However, if you try to use it directly within a Server Component, you'll see an error. This is because Next.js doesn't know `<Carousel />` is using client-only features.
   462	
   463	To fix this, you can wrap third-party components that rely on client-only features in your own Client Components:
   464	
   465	```tsx filename="app/carousel.tsx" switcher
   466	'use client'
   467	
   468	import { Carousel } from 'acme-carousel'
   469	
   470	export default Carousel
   471	```
   472	
   473	```jsx filename="app/carousel.js" switcher
   474	'use client'
   475	
   476	import { Carousel } from 'acme-carousel'
   477	
   478	export default Carousel
   479	```
   480	
   481	Now, you can use `<Carousel />` directly within a Server Component:
   482	
   483	```tsx filename="app/page.tsx" switcher
   484	import Carousel from './carousel'
   485	
   486	export default function Page() {
   487	  return (
   488	    <div>
   489	      <p>View pictures</p>
   490	      {/*  Works, since Carousel is a Client Component */}
   491	      <Carousel />
   492	    </div>
   493	  )
   494	}
   495	```
   496	
   497	```jsx filename="app/page.js" switcher
   498	import Carousel from './carousel'
   499	
   500	export default function Page() {
   501	  return (
   502	    <div>
   503	      <p>View pictures</p>
   504	      {/*  Works, since Carousel is a Client Component */}
   505	      <Carousel />
   506	    </div>
   507	  )
   508	}
   509	```
   510	
   511	> **Advice for Library Authors**
   512	>
   513	> If you’re building a component library, add the `"use client"` directive to entry points that rely on client-only features. This lets your users import components into Server Components without needing to create wrappers.
   514	>
   515	> It's worth noting some bundlers might strip out `"use client"` directives. You can find an example of how to configure esbuild to include the `"use client"` directive in the [React Wrap Balancer](https://github.com/shuding/react-wrap-balancer/blob/main/tsup.config.ts#L10-L13) and [Vercel Analytics](https://github.com/vercel/analytics/blob/main/packages/web/tsup.config.js#L26-L30) repositories.
   516	
   517	### Preventing environment poisoning
   518	
   519	JavaScript modules can be shared between both Server and Client Components modules. This means it's possible to accidentally import server-only code into the client. For example, consider the following function:
   520	
   521	```ts filename="lib/data.ts" switcher
   522	export async function getData() {
   523	  const res = await fetch('https://external-service.com/data', {
   524	    headers: {
   525	      authorization: process.env.API_KEY,
   526	    },
   527	  })
   528	
   529	  return res.json()
   530	}
   531	```
   532	
   533	```js filename="lib/data.js" switcher
   534	export async function getData() {
   535	  const res = await fetch('https://external-service.com/data', {
   536	    headers: {
   537	      authorization: process.env.API_KEY,
   538	    },
   539	  })
   540	
   541	  return res.json()
   542	}
   543	```
   544	
   545	This function contains an `API_KEY` that should never be exposed to the client.
   546	
   547	In Next.js, only environment variables prefixed with `NEXT_PUBLIC_` are included in the client bundle. If variables are not prefixed, Next.js replaces them with an empty string.
   548	
   549	As a result, even though `getData()` can be imported and executed on the client, it won't work as expected.
   550	
   551	To prevent accidental usage in Client Components, you can use the [`server-only` package](https://www.npmjs.com/package/server-only).
   552	
   553	Then, import the package into a file that contains server-only code:
   554	
   555	```js filename="lib/data.js"
   556	import 'server-only'
   557	
   558	export async function getData() {
   559	  const res = await fetch('https://external-service.com/data', {
   560	    headers: {
   561	      authorization: process.env.API_KEY,
   562	    },
   563	  })
   564	
   565	  return res.json()
   566	}
   567	```
   568	
   569	Now, if you try to import the module into a Client Component, there will be a build-time error.
   570	
   571	The corresponding [`client-only` package](https://www.npmjs.com/package/client-only) can be used to mark modules that contain client-only logic like code that accesses the `window` object.
   572	
   573	In Next.js, installing `server-only` or `client-only` is **optional**. However, if your linting rules flag extraneous dependencies, you may install them to avoid issues.
   574	
   575	```bash package="npm"
   576	npm install server-only
   577	```
   578	
   579	```bash package="yarn"
   580	yarn add server-only
   581	```
   582	
   583	```bash package="pnpm"
   584	pnpm add server-only
   585	```
   586	
   587	```bash package="bun"
   588	bun add server-only
   589	```
   590	
   591	Next.js handles `server-only` and `client-only` imports internally to provide clearer error messages when a module is used in the wrong environment. The contents of these packages from NPM are not used by Next.js.
   592	
   593	Next.js also provides its own type declarations for `server-only` and `client-only`, for TypeScript configurations where [`noUncheckedSideEffectImports`](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#noUncheckedSideEffectImports) is active.

