3. **Current Google People-Also-Ask questions checked 10 Jul 2026 ask for sleeve-vs-medication comparison**

> A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway unchanged.

> A gastric sleeve permanently reduces stomach size, a gastric bypass reduces stomach size and reroutes part of the small intestine, and prescription weight-management medicines, including GLP-1 medicines, work differently again; there is no single best option for everyone, and your surgeon can explain how reflux, BMI, diabetes, medication history and surgical risk affect the choice.

Why it matters: This answers current search demand without prescription brand names. If this comparison links to another page, use the real destination slug `/weight-loss-medication`; keep the pending TGA/legal advertising-policy decision flagged below.

4. **Current Google People-Also-Ask questions checked 10 Jul 2026 ask who is not eligible**

> Your BMI is a great starting point, however your surgeon will take the time to assess your whole health picture to determine if you are a good candidate for a gastric sleeve.

> BMI is only a starting point. For Medicare item 31575, MBS eligibility generally requires a BMI of 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. Your surgeon will also assess your medical history, surgical and anaesthetic risk, nutrition, reflux, mental health, previous weight-loss attempts and ability to commit to follow-up, and some people may need conditions managed first or may not be suitable for surgery.

Why it matters: Keep this in the eligibility section and link the anchor text "am I eligible for weight loss surgery" to the real destination slug `/am-i-eligible`. This explicitly answers who may not meet the MBS threshold or may not be suitable for surgery without making an individual eligibility determination.

5. **Current Google People-Also-Ask questions checked 10 Jul 2026 ask about the 30/30 eating rule**


4. **Current Google People-Also-Ask questions checked 10 Jul 2026 ask who is not eligible**

> Your BMI is a great starting point, however your surgeon will take the time to assess your whole health picture to determine if you are a good candidate for a gastric sleeve.

> BMI is only a starting point. For Medicare item 31575, MBS eligibility generally requires a BMI of 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. Your surgeon will also assess your medical history, surgical and anaesthetic risk, nutrition, reflux, mental health, previous weight-loss attempts and ability to commit to follow-up, and some people may need conditions managed first or may not be suitable for surgery.

Why it matters: Keep this in the eligibility section and link the anchor text "am I eligible for weight loss surgery" to the real destination slug `/am-i-eligible`. This explicitly answers who may not meet the MBS threshold or may not be suitable for surgery without making an individual eligibility determination.

5. **Current Google People-Also-Ask questions checked 10 Jul 2026 ask about the 30/30 eating rule**

6. **Long-term follow-up** — Staged eating progression (fluids → soft → regular textures) with dietitian support. Ongoing reviews and blood tests help monitor nutrition and identify issues over time.

---

### Benefits (6 bullets)
Each bullet should convey one clear, qualified benefit. Do not use superlatives.

1. Weight loss — outcomes vary between individuals; no specific result or timeline is guaranteed. verify: requires a cited clinical source + practice sign-off before publication
2. Reduced appetite — the procedure lowers ghrelin production, and some people may find smaller portions more satisfying; individual experiences vary.

